One of the most important benefits of adding healthier food options in school cafeterias is improved overall health. Eating a nutritious and balanced diet is essential for a developing body and mind, and incorporating healthier options into meal plans ensures that students are receiving the nutrients they need. Research also shows that making healthier options available increases the likelihood that students will make better eating choices. Additionally, providing healthy selections can help combat issues like childhood obesity and chronic illnesses. Another advantage of adding healthier food items to school cafeterias is that it teaches students the importance of making good nutrition choices. Instilling healthy habits in younger generations can lead to better health outcomes later in life. By exposing students to a variety of nutritious options, it can make healthy eating seem less overwhelming and more achievable. Finally, including more health options can also be an affordable way for schools to improve overall student wellbeing. Introducing cheaper, healthier items such as fruits and vegetables can have positive impacts on students’ health without significantly increasing costs. In conclusion, changing the cafeteria menu to include more healthy food options is an incredibly beneficial decision for schools and students alike. Not only does it improve students’ health, it also teaches them the importance of good nutrition and can even be a cost-effective initiative.
Dear State Senator, I am writing to you today to express my view on the Electoral College system and its significance in choosing the President of the United States. While some argue that the Electoral College is outdated and unfair, I strongly believe that it is a crucial component of our democratic system. Firstly, the Electoral College ensures that the President is elected by a broad coalition of states rather than just the most populous ones. Without the Electoral College, a candidate could win the presidency by focusing solely on densely populated areas, neglecting the needs and concerns of rural and smaller states. The winner-take-all system used in most states also means that a candidate can win an electoral vote with a minimal plurality, providing incentive for candidates to campaign in all states. Secondly, the Electoral College provides a mechanism for preserving the interests of smaller states. The total number of electoral votes is an even number, which means that a candidate can win an electoral vote with a slight plurality in a state, creating a landslide victory. This encourages candidates to campaign in all states, rather than just focusing on the most populous ones. Thirdly, the Electoral College ensures that the President has trans-regional appeal. A candidate with only regional appeal is unlikely to be a successful president, as they will have little regard for the interests of other regions. The Electoral College requires a presidential candidate to have appeal across different regions, making them more likely to be a successful and inclusive president. Fourthly, the Electoral College avoids the problem of run-off elections. In the event of a tie in the electoral vote, the House of Representatives would decide the election, which could lead to a partisan outcome. The Electoral College ens
Seeking multiple opinions when making an important decision or asking for advice is valuable because it allows someone to gain different perspectives on their situation and options. Getting input from more than one source prevents being limited to only one person's point of view or potential bias. Various people may have had different experiences that could provide useful insights. For example, if someone wanted career advice, talking to friends in their field as well as friends in other industries might give a more well-rounded picture of their possibilities. A friend in the same career could offer tips specific to that role, while others could suggest alternative paths they may not have considered. Getting perspectives from people who have walked different paths means more choices and factors can be weighed. Multiple opinions also act as a check against bad advice from any one individual. No one person has all the answers, and people can make mistakes in their suggestions. However, common themes or points that multiple sources agree on are likely more reliable recommendations. Outlier advice that only one person gave can be identified and examined more critically rather than automatically followed. Seeking various perspectives forces someone to think through their dilemma from angles they may have overlooked. Discussing it from all sides could help new ideas emerge for compromise options. If viewpoints differ significantly, it shows the decision may not have one clearly right answer and understanding all potential consequences of each choice becomes even more valuable. With a diversity of input, someone has a richer set of data to use when determining what option best fits their specific situation and goals. In summary, getting many viewpoints provides a more well-rounded analysis to base an important choice on compared to only one individual's advice. Multiple perspectives act as checks against misguidance and help explore all facets of the decision. The ability to make an informed choice increases substantially with input from a variety of sources.
Hey, Mrs. Johnson! I'm here to write about whether or not cell phones should be allowed in classrooms. I know, I know, it's a controversial topic, but hear me out. First of all, let's talk about the pros of having cell phones in classrooms. One of the biggest advantages is that they can be super helpful for learning. Like, you can look up stuff on the internet, or use apps to help you study for tests. And, like, you can use them to take notes, which is way easier than writing everything down by hand. Plus, you can play educational games on them, which can make learning more fun. But, there are also some cons to having cell phones in classrooms. For one thing, they can be super distracting. Like, you can get sucked into social media or games, and forget what you're supposed to be learning. And, like, it's not fair to the kids who don't have cell phones, 'cause they get left behind. Plus, there's the risk of cyberbullying or, like, getting hacked or something. So, I think the decision to allow cell phones in classrooms should be based on the teacher's discretion. Like, if they think it'll help the students learn, they should let them use them. But, if they think it'll be too distracting, they should probably ban them. In conclusion, cell phones can be useful in classrooms, but they can also be distracting. It's up to the teachers to decide what's best for their students. And, I'm gonna go finish my math homework now. I hope that makes sense, Mrs. Johnson! *nervous smile
Dear Principal, I am writing to express my opinion on the proposed policy change that requires students to have at least a grade B average to participate in sports or other activities. I believe that this policy change is not fair to many students who have a grade C average. Firstly, not all students are academically gifted, and they may struggle to maintain a grade B average. They may have personal or family issues that affect their academic performance. It is not fair to punish them by denying them the opportunity to participate in sports or other activities, which can be beneficial to their physical and mental health. Secondly, sports and other activities can help students to develop leadership, teamwork, and other valuable life skills. By restricting their participation based on academic grades, we may be denying them an opportunity to grow and develop as individuals. It is not fair to limit their potential by enforcing such a policy. Lastly, some students may need the motivation of sports or other activities to improve their academic performance. By denying them the opportunity to participate, we may be discouraging them from trying harder and improving their grades. In conclusion, I believe that the proposed policy change is not fair to many students and should not be implemented. Instead, we should focus on providing support and resources to help students improve their academic performance without denying them the opportunity to participate in sports or other activities. Thank you for considering my opinion. Sincerely, [Your Name]
Dear Senator, I am writing to you today to express my strong support for abolishing the Electoral College and electing the President of the United States by popular vote. The Electoral College is an outdated and undemocratic system that has no place in the 21st century. It was created in the 18th century when the United States was a very different country. At that time, communication was slow and travel was difficult. It was not practical for all citizens to vote directly for President. The Electoral College was a compromise that allowed for a national election without requiring all citizens to vote. However, the Electoral College is no longer necessary. Communication and travel are now quick and easy. All citizens have the opportunity to vote directly for President. There is no reason to continue with an outdated system that disenfranchises millions of Americans. The Electoral College also gives disproportionate power to small states. A candidate can win the popular vote but lose the election if they do not win enough electoral votes. This has happened five times in American history, most recently in 2016. In 2016, Hillary Clinton won the popular vote by nearly 3 million votes, but Donald Trump won the election by winning the Electoral College. This is simply not fair. The Electoral College also encourages candidates to focus their campaigns on a few swing states. This means that they ignore large parts of the country, including entire states. This is not only unfair to those voters, but it also means that candidates are not held accountable to the entire country. I urge you to support a constitutional amendment to abolish the Electoral College and elect the President by popular vote. This is the only way to ensure that all Americans have an equal voice in our democracy. Thank you for your time and consideration. Sincerely, [Your Name]
In recent years, there has been a dramatic shift in people's perspectives on how to live life. More and more, people are beginning to realize the importance of taking risks and making their own decisions in order to gain a better understanding of life. It is believed that by taking risks and having the courage to take control of one's own life, an individual will be more likely to realize their true potential and pursue a life of happiness and fulfillment. Additionally, it is important to remember that one must learn to love oneself and depend on themselves in order to be truly happy with their lives. Having the confidence to face any challenge and take full responsibility for one's own actions will lead to a better, more sustainable perspective on life. Whether in personal or professional matters, being able to take risks and effectively decide one's own path is a key element to a successful life. It is clear that taking risks and making sound decisions is an essential part in gaining a better perspective on life. To do this one must learn to depend on oneself and learn to accept the consequences of their actions. By doing this, a person will have more control over their own life and be able to pursue a higher level of happiness.
I believe that reducing meat consumption is a good idea due to environmental and health concerns. Therefore, I am in favor of adopting a vegetarian or vegan diet. In this essay, I will present my reasons and examples to justify my position. Firstly, reducing meat consumption can help reduce the environmental impact of the meat industry. The meat industry is a significant contributor to greenhouse gas emissions, deforestation, and water pollution. For example, according to the United Nations, the meat industry is responsible for 14.5% of global greenhouse gas emissions. By reducing meat consumption, we can help reduce the environmental impact of the meat industry and contribute to a more sustainable future. Secondly, a vegetarian or vegan diet can have significant health benefits. Studies have shown that a plant-based diet can lower the risk of heart disease, stroke, and some types of cancer. Additionally, a plant-based diet can help with weight management and improve overall health. For example, according to the American Heart Association, a vegetarian diet can lower the risk of heart disease by up to 40%. However, some argue that a vegetarian or vegan diet lacks essential nutrients such as protein and iron. While it is true that meat is a good source of protein and iron, it is possible to obtain these nutrients from plant-based sources. For example, lentils, tofu, and quinoa are good sources of protein, while spinach and fortified cereals are good sources of iron. In conclusion, reducing meat consumption and adopting a vegetarian or vegan diet can have significant environmental and health benefits. While some argue that a vegetarian or vegan diet lacks essential nutrients, it is possible to obtain these nutrients from plant-based sources. Therefore, I believe that adopting a vegetarian or vegan diet is a good idea for those who are concerned about the environment and their health.
Homework clubs offer a valuable resource to students and teachers alike. For students, having access to dedicated time and support to tackle challenging homework can result in improved understanding of concepts and improved in-class performance. For teachers, a homework club provides a platform to provide extra support to students, as well as monitor student progress, allowing them to identify and address any areas of difficulty. Additionally, students who participate in these clubs can benefit from increased motivation and a feeling of being part of a supportive team. Homework clubs thus represent a viable and potentially beneficial solution to meet the needs of both students and teachers.
Dear Senator, I am writing to you today to express my support for keeping the Electoral College as the method of electing the President of the United States. I believe that the Electoral College is a fair and democratic system that ensures that all states are represented in the election of the President. The Electoral College is a system in which each state is allocated a number of electors equal to its number of Senators and Representatives in Congress. This means that smaller states have a smaller number of electors, while larger states have a larger number of electors. This system ensures that all states have a say in the election of the President, even if they have a smaller population than other states. One of the biggest arguments in favor of the Electoral College is that it prevents a few large states from dominating the election of the President. If the election were decided by popular vote, then candidates would only need to campaign in the most populous states in order to win the election. This would leave smaller states with little to no representation in the White House. The Electoral College also helps to ensure that the President is someone who can appeal to a broad range of voters. In order to win the Electoral College, a candidate must win a majority of votes in a majority of states. This means that a candidate cannot simply win the election by appealing to a small group of voters in a few states. I believe that the Electoral College is a fair and democratic system that ensures that all states are represented in the election of the President. I urge you to vote against any proposals to abolish the Electoral College. Sincerely, [Your Name]
[Your name] [Grade 7] [School name] [Date] [Principal's name] [School name] [Address] Dear Mrs. [Principal’s name], I am writing this letter to share my thoughts about the school’s cell phone policy. I appreciate the effort you have taken to reconsider it. However, I firmly believe that allowing students to bring their phones to school and use them during free periods is the best policy. I am writing this letter to convince you why this policy is better than the other. Firstly, allowing students to bring their phones will increase their responsibility. It will teach them how to use their phones wisely and in the right way. It can also help them to manage their time between studying and using their phones. If we are allowed to use our phones during break time, we will be able to connect with our families and friends easily. Moreover, we can use our phones for educational purposes, to access the internet or to use educational apps. Secondly, in case of emergencies, it will be very helpful to have phones with us. We can contact our parents or guardians in case of any emergency without having to borrow anyone's phone. On the other hand, if the school implements a "No phones" policy, it will be inconvenient for many students. It may also create a feeling of frustration and anger. In addition, some students may have medical conditions that require them to have a phone at all times. In conclusion, I would like to request you to please consider my thoughts about the cell phone policy at our school. I believe that allowing students to bring their phones to school will be beneficial for everyone. Thank you for considering my request. Yours sincerely, [Your name]
Hey, I'm totally stoked to be writing this essay! 🚨👮♂️ So, I've been thinking a lot about my future lately, and I've decided that I want to be a police officer when I grow up! 💪 Like, can you imagine being able to protect and serve your community every day? It's like, the ultimate goal! 🙌 I've started doing some research on careers in the police force, and let me tell you, it's like, super interesting! 🤯 Did you know that there are so many different types of police officers? Like, there's patrol officers, detectives, SWAT team members, and even K-9 officers! 🐕👀 I'm also really into criminology classes right now. I think it's so cool to learn about the psychology of criminals and how they think. It's like, mind-blowing! 🤯 Plus, it's really helpful for understanding how to catch them and keep our communities safe! 💯 Oh, and I almost forgot! 😅 I'm totally gonna write a letter to my local police department expressing my interest in visiting and learning more about their work. I think it would be so awesome to see the police in action and learn from them directly! 🤝 So, yeah, that's my essay! I'm super excited to explore this career path and see where it takes me! 🚀 Thanks for reading! 👏
Emerson believed in the power of expansion - he believed that a person who had already mastered a skill should constantly expand their horizons and acquire new skills. I agree, as I think doing so can help open up new opportunities, and provide a sense of satisfaction and accomplishment. Additionally, expanding one’s horizons allows for a better understanding of how different skills fit together. For example, if someone has mastered the art of computer programming, but decides to also learn web design, they may be able to see ways in which the two skills can combine and create something completely new. That said, expanding horizons can also have its challenges. If someone has already mastered a skill, and is used to succeeding quickly, learning something new can be difficult and frustrating if progress is slow. Additionally, it can be difficult to dedicate the time needed to learn something new when someone is already busy with their job and personal life. Ultimately, I believe expanding one’s horizons through learning something new can be beneficial, as long as one approaches it with a positive mindset and is patient with the progress. There may be challenges, but the potential rewards can be incredibly worthwhile.
When people seek advice, it can greatly benefit them in making better decisions, providing guidance to others, and improving their own lives. When someone is offering you advice, it's important to listen to them because it could have helped them in their own life and could potentially help you as well. Additionally, advice from family, friends, and other trusted individuals can be invaluable in navigating life's challenges. As someone who has given advice to others, I can attest to the positive impact it can have on their lives. For example, I have given advice to my younger siblings to help them excel in school and have seen firsthand the improvements in their grades and confidence as a result. Additionally, I have given advice to friends who were going through difficult times and have seen them overcome their challenges and move forward in a more positive direction. Furthermore, receiving advice can be incredibly helpful in one's own life. After listening to advice from others, I have made better decisions and have been able to navigate life's challenges with greater ease. As a result, I have been able to help others with advice and have seen the positive impact it can have on their lives. In conclusion, seeking and giving advice can greatly benefit individuals in making better decisions, providing guidance to others, and improving their own lives. As someone who has given advice, I have seen firsthand the positive impact it can have on others and have been able to help them make better choices in their own lives.
Gun control laws have been a major issue in many countries. Some people believe that stricter gun control laws are necessary to reduce the number of gun-related deaths and crimes, while others think that gun control laws only disarm law-abiding citizens and do not prevent criminals from obtaining firearms. In my opinion, I am against stricter gun control laws because they violate the Second Amendment of the Constitution, which guarantees citizens the right to bear arms. Firstly, stricter gun control laws would only disarm law-abiding citizens, which would leave them defenseless against criminals who do not follow the law. Criminals do not care about the law and will obtain firearms no matter what laws are in place. Therefore, strict gun control laws only hurt law-abiding citizens who want to protect themselves and their families. Secondly, stricter gun control laws would not reduce the number of gun-related deaths and crimes. Criminals are going to obtain firearms regardless of the laws in place. For example, in the city of Chicago, which has some of the strictest gun control laws in the United States, there are still high rates of gun-related deaths and crimes. Therefore, stricter gun control laws would not solve the problem. Lastly, the Second Amendment guarantees citizens the right to bear arms. Gun control laws violate this right, which is a fundamental right in a free society. The government should not be allowed to take away a citizen's right to bear arms. In conclusion, stricter gun control laws do not solve the problem of gun-related deaths and crimes. They only disarm law-abiding citizens and violate their constitutional rights. Gun control laws will not prevent criminals from obtaining firearms, and citizens have the right to bear arms for their own protection. Therefore, I am against stricter gun control laws.
Extending the school day by two hours has the potential to be highly beneficial to the environment. Students and teachers alike can use the additional hours to learn about energy conservation and other sustainable practices. These practices can then be implemented in the classroom and in daily life outside of school. Energy is the most precious resource on Earth. Proper energy conservation can reduce the consumption of natural resources and help protect the environment. Through energy efficient methods such as turning off lights and electronics when not in use, using laptops instead of desktop computers, and replacing incandescent bulbs with LEDs, students and teachers can reduce the overall energy consumption of the school. Furthermore, the saving of energy not only incurs monetary benefits, but can also help reduce air pollution and global warming. In addition to energy conservation, students and teachers can use the two extra hours to gain a better understanding of the importance of preserving natural resources and making the world better. By introducing activities such as recycling, composting, and upcycling, everyone can contribute in small and large ways to decrease waste and keep the planet healthy. The extra two hours of school could not only help conserve energy and resources, but can also aid in creating a better world. Through educating students in energy-saving practices, they can then take the lessons they have learned home and spread the conservation mentality to others, thereby setting an example that will ultimately result in a more positive environment for generations to come. Similarly, teaching students the importance of preserving natural resources will help foster eco-friendly habits that will have a lasting impact and create a positive environmental legacy. Overall, extending the school day by two hours presents numerous benefits to saving energy and resources and helps to make the world a better place. With continued cooperation from students and teachers, it is clear that a brighter environmental future is well within reach.
Extending the school day by two hours would cause a number of problems for students at Generic_School. First, many students rely on after-school activities to develop important skills, such as sports, music, theater, and other extracurriculars. With two more hours of classes and homework, these activities would have to be put by the wayside in order to have enough time to complete the necessary course work. Second, student health and safety could be affected by the extended school day. Going to school for an additional two hours means students would not only be awake and working longer in order to stay on top of their workload, but they would also be at increased risk of exhaustion and burnout due to the lack of time for rest and recovery. Finally, a longer school day would take away from students’ precious time spent with families and friends. Time outside of classroom boundaries is essential for students, as it allows them to relax, explore their interests, and engage in meaningful relationships. For these reasons, Generic_School should not extend the school day by two hours.
Extracurricular activities can offer a wealth of benefits to students, including improving academic performance, developing social skills, and providing a positive outlet for energy. On the other hand, students may not have the time or resources available to participate in these activities. In terms of benefits, research has demonstrated that students who are involved in extracurricular activities often have improved academic performance due to increased focus and motivation. It is also an effective way for students to develop social skills in a safe and supportive environment. Finally, extracurricular activities can provide a positive outlet for energy, resulting in improved mental and physical wellbeing. On the other hand, extracurricular activities may not be feasible for all students. Time constraints, such as having to work or care for family members, can hinder a student’s ability to participate. Additionally, some students may not be able to afford required fees or equipment. Finally, research has shown that extracurricular activities can place pressure on students, resulting in stress and other negative psychological effects. In conclusion, while extracurricular activities can offer a variety of benefits, students should be mindful of the potential drawbacks that could arise from participating.
From my perspective, I believe that it is unlikely to change someone's initial impression of you, as there are several factors that make it difficult to do so. People tend to form their first impressions quickly and based on limited information, which can be difficult to alter. For example, when I went to a networking event a few years ago, I was meeting a lot of new people from different backgrounds. Despite my best efforts to make a good impression, I could tell that some people had already formed their opinions of me before I even had a chance to speak with them. Even after I had the opportunity to interact with these individuals and demonstrate my skills and abilities, their initial impressions seemed to remain unchanged. It was frustrating, as I knew that I was capable of doing a great job, but it seemed like people were not willing to give me a chance to prove myself. In my experience, people's initial impressions can be difficult to change, regardless of whether they are positive or negative. It takes time and effort to build a relationship and establish trust, and it can be challenging to overcome someone's preconceived notions about you. Of course, there are some situations where it may be possible to change someone's initial impression, such as when you have a lot of time and opportunities to interact with them and demonstrate your value. However, in many cases, it may be more effective to focus on building a strong relationship and showing your true abilities rather than trying to change someone's initial impression.
Car-Free Cities: Limiting Car Usage for a Better Future Limiting car usage has numerous advantages for our environment, health, and communities. Recent studies and events demonstrate that people are gradually shifting towards car-free living, and it's essential to understand the benefits of this trend. In "Car-Free Cities," two news articles and a research paper provide compelling evidence for limiting car usage. Firstly, reducing car usage can significantly improve air quality and reduce carbon emissions. In Paris, where smog reached dangerous levels, the government enforced a partial driving ban, resulting in a 60% decrease in congestion (Duffer). Similarly, in Bogota, Colombia, a car-free day led to a reduction in air pollution and a positive response from citizens (Selsky). These examples demonstrate that limiting car usage can have a profound impact on air quality, making our cities healthier and more livable. Secondly, car-free living can promote physical activity and community engagement. In "The End of Car Culture," sociologist Mimi Sheller argues that the rise of car-sharing programs and bike-sharing services reflects a shift towards more flexible and active commuting arrangements (Rosenthal). By promoting alternative modes of transportation, we can encourage physical activity and foster a sense of community among citizens. Thirdly, limiting car usage can reduce traffic congestion and improve road safety. In "Car-Free Day is Spinning into a Big Hit in Bogota," the city's mayor, Antanas Mockus, notes that the absence of cars during the car-free day led to a more peaceful and enjoyable urban environment (Selsky). Similarly, in "Paris bans driving due to smog," the French government enforced a partial driving ban to alleviate traffic cong
Hey, I'm like, totally stoked to be writing this essay! 😃 So, you know how some students can graduate early? Like, a year or two early? I think it's like, totally cool! 🤯 But, there are also some drawbacks, you know? Like, what if they're not ready for college or the workforce? 🤔 So, let's talk about the benefits first! If students can graduate early, they can like, get a head start on their college career or job prospects! 🎉 They can take college classes during high school, which means they can save time and money in the long run. Plus, they can like, get a taste of college life and figure out what they want to study before everyone else! 🤓 It's like, super beneficial! 💡 But, there are also some risks, you know? Like, what if they're not ready for college? 😕 They might struggle in their classes, or they might not be able to handle the workload. And what if they're not ready for the workforce? 🤔 They might not have the skills or experience they need to land a job. It's like, important to make sure they're really ready before they graduate early! 💯 So, like, in conclusion, graduating early can be totally awesome, but we gotta make sure students are ready for the next step! 😃 It's like, totally important to consider the benefits and drawbacks before making a decision. But, if they're ready and they're gonna take advantage of the opportunities, it can be like, super beneficial! 💯🎉
Dear Mrs. Smith, I know your thinking about changing the policy so that we have to have a least a B average to play sports or do other activities. I think this is a bad idea and I'll tell you why. First of all, not every one is good at school. Some people are really good at sports or music or art, but they might not be so good at school. If they can't play sports or do other things they like, they might get discouraged and stop trying altogether. Second, having a C average is still pretty good! It's not like were failing or anything. And some people might be trying really hard and still only get a C. That's not fair to punish them for something they can't control. Third, sports and other activities are important too! They help us learn teamwork and discipline and how to work hard. They also help us relax and have fun. Taking that away from us just because were not the best at school isn't right. I think you should reconsider this idea and find a different way to help students do better in school. Maybe you could give us more help or support, or find a way to make school more interesting and fun. Thank you for listening to my opinion. Sincerely, Jane Doe
While summer is meant to be a break from formal schooling, having some structured learning over the summer can help prevent students from losing ground academically. However, summer projects should be student-designed rather than teacher-designed to maximize student buy-in and engagement. If teachers design mandatory summer work, it risks feeling like an extension of the regular school year rather than a break. Students may resent doing assignments they did not choose and see it as homework rather than an opportunity. This could ultimately undermine the goals of the projects by discouraging students from putting in full effort. In contrast, letting students have autonomy in shaping their own summer learning allows them to pursue topics they find genuinely interesting. Students should be given guidelines on types of activities or amount of time spent on a project, but flexibility to choose the specific focus. For example, they could choose to learn about a new hobby, research a country for a family heritage project, or shadow a local professional in a field of interest. Giving choice empowers students and prompts intrinsic rather than extrinsic motivation to learn. It also allows them to study things personally relevant to their lives outside of school. Teachers should be available for guidance and approval of student project proposals, rather than determining the work themselves. They can help steer students towards educational pursuits without stripping away ownership. Finished work could be optionally shared with the teacher for input or assessment. But the driving force is students' own curiosity and interests rather than mandatory assignments. In conclusion, while summer projects have value in reinforcing learning, the most effective approach is to let students design their own based on guidelines. This balances educational goals with allowing freedom and choice, maximizing student ownership over their work. A student-designed model keeps summer learning meaningful rather than a dreaded requirement.
