Dear Principal, I think that policy 1 is better because it allows us to use our phones during lunch and free times. This way, we can communicate with our friends and family, and also use our phones for educational purposes. For example, we can use our phones to look up information for a project or to access online resources for homework. Also, by allowing us to have our phones during lunch, we can take pictures of our food and share it with our friends and family, which can help us to connect with them better. Additionally, if there is an emergency, we can use our phones to contact our parents or guardians quickly. I understand that some people might say that having phones at school can be distracting, but I think that if we are responsible and turn off our phones during class time, it won't be a problem. I believe that policy 1 is a good balance between allowing us to use our phones for useful purposes and still maintaining a focused learning environment. Thank you for considering our opinions. Sincerely, [Your Name]
As an 8th-grade student, I have mastered the skill of playing the piano. I have been playing for the past five years and have taken lessons for the past three. I have learned how to read music, play different styles of music, and even compose my own pieces. My goal is to improve my piano skills by working on my technique and speed. I want to be able to play more complex pieces with ease and fluidity. To reach this goal, I plan on practicing for at least 30 minutes a day, working on specific exercises to improve my technique and speed. I will also try to play more challenging pieces and work on my sight-reading skills. Despite the challenges I may face, I will persevere and continue to challenge myself. I believe that growth and progress are important as a person, and that it is essential to constantly strive for improvement. By working hard and pushing myself, I can become an even better pianist and achieve my goal of mastering the piano. In conclusion, mastering a skill is an important aspect of personal growth and development. By setting a goal and taking steps to reach it, we can improve our skills and become better versions of ourselves. It is important to persevere and challenge ourselves, even in the face of difficulty, as this will lead to growth and progress. As an 8th-grade student, I am grateful for the opportunity to learn and improve my piano skills, and I look forward to continuing this journey of growth and development.
